\cf2 Associativity defines how operators of the same precedence level are grouped into expressions. The table indicates whether operators of a given level are left- or right-associative. For example, multiplication and division are left associative, so the following expression results in a value of 3:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 3\cf16  \cf5 *\cf16  \cf14 4\cf16  \cf5 /\cf16  \cf14 2\cf16  \cf5 /\cf16  \cf14 2\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 It is equivalent to the following parenthesized expression to explicitly control the order of evaluation:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 ((\cf14 3\cf16  \cf5 *\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 /\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 /\cf16  \cf14 2\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

