\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 Mojo also uses the caret (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ^
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 ) as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#transfer-arguments-var-and-"}}{\fldrslt \ul transfer sigil}}. In expressions where its use might be ambiguous, Mojo treats the character as the bitwise XOR operator. For example, 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 x^+1
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  is treated as 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 (x)^(+1)
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Arithmetic and bitwise operators\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/types/#numeric-types"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs32 \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Numeric types}}
\fs32  describes the different numeric types provided by the Mojo standard library. The arithmetic and bitwise operators have slightly different behavior depending on the types of values provided.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Int/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Int}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/uint/UInt/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 UInt}} types represent signed and unsigned integers of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_(computer_architecture)"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 word size}} of the CPU, typically 64 bits or 32 bits.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  types support all arithmetic operators except matrix multiplication (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), as well as all bitwise and shift operators. If both operands to a binary operator are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values the result is an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , if both operands are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values the result is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and if one operand is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and the other 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  the result is an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  a_int\cf5 :\cf16  Int \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 7\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  b_int\cf5 :\cf16  Int \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 4\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 sum_int \cf5 =\cf16  a_int \cf5 +\cf16  b_int  \cf6 # Result is type Int\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "Int sum:"\cf5 ,\cf16  sum_int\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  i_uint\cf5 :\cf16  UInt \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 9\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  j_uint\cf5 :\cf16  UInt \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 8\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 sum_uint \cf5 =\cf16  i_uint \cf5 +\cf16  j_uint  \cf6 # Result is type UInt\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "UInt sum:"\cf5 ,\cf16  sum_uint\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 sum_mixed \cf5 =\cf16  a_int \cf5 +\cf16  Int\cf5 (\cf16 i_uint\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # Result is type Int\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "Mixed sum:"\cf5 ,\cf16  sum_mixed\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 quotient_int \cf5 =\cf16  a_int \cf5 //\cf16  b_int\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "Int quotient:"\cf5 ,\cf16  quotient_int\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 quotient_uint \cf5 =\cf16  i_uint \cf5 /\cf16  j_uint\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "UInt quotient:"\cf5 ,\cf16  quotient_uint\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

