\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo standard library defines the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/SIMD/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 SIMD}} type to represent a fixed-size array of values that can fit into a processor's register. This allows you to take advantage of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_instruction,_multiple_data"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 single instruction, multiple data}} operations in hardware to efficiently process multiple values in parallel. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values of a numeric {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/dtype/DType/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 DType}} support all arithmetic operators except for matrix multiplication (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), though the left shift (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <<
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and right shift (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 >>
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) operators support only integral types. Additionally, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values of an integral or boolean type support all bitwise operators. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values apply the operators in an elementwise fashion, as shown in the following example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 simd1 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 int32\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](\cf14 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 5\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd2 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 int32\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](-\cf14 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd3 \cf5 =\cf16  simd1 \cf5 *\cf16  simd2\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 simd3\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 [-2, 6, -12, 20]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Scalar}}
\fs32 \cf2  values are simply aliases for single-element 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vectors, so 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float16
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is just an alias for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[DType.float16, 1]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Therefore 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Scalar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values support the same set of arithmetic and bitwise operators.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  f1\cf5 :\cf16  Float16 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 2.5\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  f2\cf5 :\cf16  Float16 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 4.0\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  f3 \cf5 =\cf16  f1 \cf5 *\cf16  f2  \cf6 # Implicitly of type Float16\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 f3\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 -10.0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When using these operators on 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values, Mojo requires both to have the same size and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and the result is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  of the same size and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The operators do not automatically widen lower precision 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values to higher precision. This means that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  of each value must be the same or else the result is a compilation error.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  i8\cf5 :\cf16  Int8 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 8\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  f64\cf5 :\cf16  Float64 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 64.0\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 result \cf5 =\cf16  i8 \cf5 *\cf16  f64\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 error: invalid call to '__mul__': failed to infer parameter 'type' of parent struct 'SIMD'\cb1 \
\cb15     result = i8 * f64\cb1 \
\cb15              ~~~^~~~~\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you need to perform an arithmetic or bitwise operator on two 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values of different types, you can explicitly convert a value to the desired type either by invoking its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/SIMD/#cast"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 cast()}} method or by passing it as an argument to the constructor of the target type.\
For example, to fix the previous example, add an explicit conversion:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  i8\cf5 :\cf16  Int8 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 8\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  f64\cf5 :\cf16  Float64 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 64.0\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 result \cf5 =\cf16  Float64\cf5 (\cf16 i8\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 *\cf16  f64\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here are some more examples of converting SIMD values using both constructors and the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 simd4 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 float32\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](\cf14 2.2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 3.3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4.4\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 5.5\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd5 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 int16\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](-\cf14 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd6 \cf5 =\cf16  simd4 \cf5 *\cf16  simd5\cf5 .\cf16 cast\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 float32\cf5 ]()\cf16   \cf6 # Convert with cast() method\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd6:"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd6\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd7 \cf5 =\cf16  simd5 \cf5 +\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 int16\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](\cf16 simd4\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # Convert with SIMD constructor\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd7:"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd7\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 simd6: [-2.2, 6.6, -13.200001, 22.0]\cb1 \
\cb15 simd7: [1, 5, 1, 9]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 One exception is that the exponentiation operator, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 **
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , is overloaded so that you can specify an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type exponent. All values in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are exponentiated to the same power.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 base_simd \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 float64\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](\cf14 1.1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2.2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 3.3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4.4\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  power\cf5 :\cf16  Int \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 2\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 pow_simd \cf5 =\cf16  base_simd \cf5 **\cf16  power  \cf6 # Result is SIMD[DType.float64, 4]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 pow_simd\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 [1.2100000000000002, 4.8400000000000007, 10.889999999999999, 19.360000000000003]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are three operators related to division:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
/
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the "true division" operator, performs floating point division for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values with a floating point 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values with an integral 
