\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 IntLiteral
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 FloatLiteral
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int_literal/IntLiteral/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 IntLiteral}}
\fs32  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/float_literal/FloatLiteral/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 FloatLiteral}} are compile-time, numeric values. When they are used in a compile-time context, they are arbitrary-precision values. When they are used in a run-time context, they are materialized as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type values, respectively.\
As an example, the following code causes a compile-time error because the calculated 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is too large to store in an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15  \cf5 comptime\cf16  big_int \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 (\cf14 1\cf16  \cf5 <<\cf16  \cf14 65\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  \cf14 123456789\cf16   \cf6 # IntLiteral\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  too_big_int\cf5 :\cf16  Int \cf5 =\cf16  big_int\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "Result:"\cf5 ,\cf16  too_big_int\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 note: integer value 36893488147542560021 requires 67 bits to store, but the destination bit width is only 64 bits wide\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 However in the following example, taking that same 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value, dividing by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  10 and then assigning the result to an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable compiles and runs successfully, because the final 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  quotient can fit in a 64-bit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
