\fs40 \cf2 Operating on non-boolean values with boolean-returning operators\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Many of Mojo's word-based operators (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 in
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 not in
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is not
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 not
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 and
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 or
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) return booleans without their arguments being boolean themselves.\
For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 in
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 not in
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are used with values and collections:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  \cf22 list\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf14 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 3\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf14 1\cf16  \cf5 in\cf16  \cf22 list\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # True, `in` operator checks for membership in the list\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf14 4\cf16  \cf5 in\cf16  \cf22 list\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # False, `in` operator\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf14 4\cf16  \cf5 not\cf16  \cf5 in\cf16  \cf22 list\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # True, `not in` operator\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Some types have identities that can be tested with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 not is
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Pointers can use their unique addresses to distinguish one allocation from another:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb15 # Identity, Wrapped pointers\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 MemoryHolder\cf5 (\cf16 ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  p\cf5 :\cf16  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ,\cf16  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __init__\cf5 (out\cf16  self\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 p \cf5 =\cf16  alloc\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ](\cf14 1\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __is__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  other\cf5 :\cf16  MemoryHolder\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  Bool\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 p \cf5 ==\cf16  other\cf5 .\cf16 p\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __isnot__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  other\cf5 :\cf16  MemoryHolder\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  Bool\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  \cf5 not\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 __is__\cf5 (\cf16 other\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf16  self\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 p\cf5 .\cf16 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 p\cf5 .\cf16 free\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  p1 \cf5 =\cf16  MemoryHolder\cf5 ();\cf16  \cf5 var\cf16  p2 \cf5 =\cf16  MemoryHolder\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 p1 \cf5 is\cf16  p2\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # False, two pointer allocations\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 p1 \cf5 is\cf16  \cf5 not\cf16  p2\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # True, two pointer allocations\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 p1 \cf5 is\cf16  p1\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # True, same pointer allocation\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Boolable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  types adopt a kind of truthiness. You can use them directly in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 -statements or convert them to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  s1 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "Hello"\cf5 ;\cf16  \cf5 var\cf16  s2 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 ""\cf16  \cf6 # Empty strings are Falsy\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 Bool\cf5 (not\cf16  s1\cf5 ))\cf16   \cf6 # False, non-empty string is Truthy\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 Bool\cf5 (not\cf16  s2\cf5 ))\cf16   \cf6 # True, empty string is Falsy\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 Bool\cf5 (\cf16 s1 \cf5 and\cf16  s2\cf5 ))\cf16  \cf6 # False\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 Bool\cf5 (\cf16 s1 \cf5 and\cf16  s1\cf5 ))\cf16  \cf6 # True\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 Bool\cf5 (\cf16 s1 \cf5 or\cf16  s2\cf5 ))\cf16  \cf6 # True\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 Bool\cf5 (\cf16 s2 \cf5 or\cf16  s2\cf5 ))\cf16  \cf6 # False\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Integers are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Boolable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so you can use them with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 and
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 or
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . In this example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0 or 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides a boolean check on whether a function can compile. 0 is falsy and 1 is truthy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 # Change the constraint to 0 to activate the error\cb1 \
\cb15 def test() where 0 or 1\cb1 \
\cb15     print("This should be compiled, the condition is true")\cb1 \
\cb15 test()  # This should be compiled, the condition is true\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Elementwise comparisons\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For elementwise comparisons that return a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[DType.bool]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  result, use the comparison methods: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 eq()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ne()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 lt()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 le()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gt()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ge()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . These methods work with both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 -to-
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 -to-scalar comparisons. Here are examples showing all six elementwise comparison methods:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 simd8 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 int32\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](\cf14 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd9 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 int32\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](\cf14 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd8.eq(simd9):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd8\cf5 .\cf16 eq\cf5 (\cf16 simd9\cf5 ))\cf16     \cf6 # Equal\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd8.ne(simd9):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd8\cf5 .\cf16 ne\cf5 (\cf16 simd9\cf5 ))\cf16     \cf6 # Not equal\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd8.lt(simd9):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd8\cf5 .\cf16 lt\cf5 (\cf16 simd9\cf5 ))\cf16     \cf6 # Less than\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd8.le(simd9):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd8\cf5 .\cf16 le\cf5 (\cf16 simd9\cf5 ))\cf16     \cf6 # Less than or equal\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd8.gt(simd9):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd8\cf5 .\cf16 gt\cf5 (\cf16 simd9\cf5 ))\cf16     \cf6 # Greater than\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd8.ge(simd9):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd8\cf5 .\cf16 ge\cf5 (\cf16 simd9\cf5 ))\cf16     \cf6 # Greater than or equal\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 simd8.eq(simd9): [True, True, False, True]\cb1 \
\cb15 simd8.ne(simd9): [False, False, True, False]\cb1 \
\cb15 simd8.lt(simd9): [False, False, True, False]\cb1 \
\cb15 simd8.le(simd9): [True, True, True, True]\cb1 \
\cb15 simd8.gt(simd9): [False, False, False, False]\cb1 \
\cb15 simd8.ge(simd9): [True, True, False, True]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also use these methods for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 -to-scalar comparisons:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 simd4 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 int16\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](-\cf14 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd5 \cf5 =\cf16  simd4\cf5 .\cf16 gt\cf5 (\cf14 2\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # SIMD[DType.bool, 4]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd4.gt(2):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd5\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 simd6 \cf5 =\cf16  SIMD\cf5 [\cf16 DType\cf5 .\cf16 float32\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 4\cf5 ](\cf14 1.1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 2.2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 3.3\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 4.4\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 simd7 \cf5 =\cf16  simd6\cf5 .\cf16 gt\cf5 (\cf14 0.5\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # SIMD[DType.bool, 4]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "simd6.gt(0.5):"\cf5 ,\cf16  simd7\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 simd4.gt(2): [False, False, False, True]\cb1 \
\cb15 simd6.gt(0.5): [True, False, True, False]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use elementwise comparison methods when you need to compare each element individually and work with the resulting boolean mask for further processing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 String operators\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As discussed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/types/#strings"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Strings}}, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string/String/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul String}} type represents a mutable string value. In contrast, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/string_literal/StringLiteral/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul StringLiteral}} type represents a literal string that is embedded into your compiled program, but at run-time it materializes to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , allowing you to mutate it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 message \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "Hello"\cf16        \cf6 # type = String\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  name \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 " Pat"\cf16        \cf6 # type = StringLiteral\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 greeting \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 " good Day!"\cf16   \cf6 # type = String\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf6 \cb15 # Mutate the original `message` String\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 message \cf5 +=\cf16  name\cb1 \
\cb15 message \cf5 +=\cf16  greeting\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 message\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Hello Pat good day!\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This means that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values can be intermixed with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values in any runtime expression without having to convert between types.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 String concatenation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator performs string concatenation. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type supports compile-time string concatenation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  last_name \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "Curie"\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf6 \cb15 # Compile-time StringLiteral value\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf16  marie \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "Marie "\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  last_name\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 marie\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf6 \cb15 # Compile-time concatenation before materializing to a run-time `String`\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 pierre \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "Pierre "\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  last_name\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 pierre\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 When concatenating multiple values together to form a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 String
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 , using the multi-argument 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 String()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  constructor is more performant than using multiple 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 +
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  concatenation operators and can improve code readability. For example, instead of writing this:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf16 \cb20 result \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "The point at ("\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  String\cf5 (\cf16 x\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  \cf21 ", "\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  String\cf5 (\cf16 y\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  \cf21 ")"\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 you can write:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf16 \cb20 result \cf5 =\cf16  String\cf5 (\cf21 "The point at ("\cf5 ,\cf16  x\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf21 ", "\cf5 ,\cf16  y\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf21 ")"\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
