\fs48 \cf2 In-place assignment operators\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mutable types that support binary arithmetic, bitwise, and shift operators typically support equivalent in-place assignment operators. That means that for a type that supports the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator, the following two statements are essentially equivalent:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 a \cf5 =\cf16  a \cf5 +\cf16  b\cb1 \
\cb15 a \cf5 +=\cf16  b\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However there is a subtle difference between the two. In the first example, the expression 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a + b
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  produces a new value, which is then assigned to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . In contrast, the second example does an in-place modification of the value currently assigned to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/#register-passable"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul register-passable types}}, the compiled results might be equivalent at run-time. But for a memory-only type, the first example allocates storage for the result of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a + b
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and then assigns the value to the variable, whereas the second example can do an in-place modification of the existing value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 A type must explicitly implement in-place assignment methods, so you might encounter some types where in-place equivalents are not supported.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Conditional expressions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, as in Python, a conditional expression enables you to select a value from one of two options:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb23 Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 result \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "pass"\cf16  \cf5 if\cf16  score \cf5 >\cf16  \cf14 65\cf16  \cf5 else\cf16  \cf21 "fail"\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This pattern can be used in expressions, assignments, and as function arguments:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 exp \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 5\cf16  \cf6 # or -5\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 result \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 2.0\cf16  \cf5 **\cf16  Float64\cf5 (\cf16 exp\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 if\cf16  exp \cf5 >=\cf16  \cf14 0\cf16  \\\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 else\cf16  \cf14 1.0\cf16  \cf5 /\cf16  \cf5 (\cf14 2.0\cf16  \cf5 **\cf16  \cf22 abs\cf5 (\cf16 Float64\cf5 (\cf16 exp\cf5 )))\cf16  \\\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # For 5, 32, for -5, 0.03125\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 greet\cf5 (\cf21 "Sami"\cf16  \cf5 if\cf16  is_sami \cf5 else\cf16  \cf21 "Cass"\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Assignment expressions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The "walrus" operator, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 :=
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , allows you to assign a value to a variable within an expression. The value provided is both assigned to the variable and becomes the result of the expression. This often can simplify conditional or looping logic. For example, consider the following prompting loop:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 while\cf16  \cf14 True\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     name \cf5 =\cf16  \cf22 input\cf5 (\cf21 "Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: "\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 if\cf16  name \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf21 "quit"\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 break\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf21 "Hello,"\cf5 ,\cf16  name\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: Coco\cb1 \
\cb15 Hello, Coco\cb1 \
\cb15 Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: Vivienne\cb1 \
\cb15 Hello, Vivienne\cb1 \
\cb15 Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: quit\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Using the walrus operator, you can implement the same behavior like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 while\cf16  \cf5 (\cf16 name \cf5 :=\cf16  \cf22 input\cf5 (\cf21 "Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: "\cf5 ))\cf16  \cf5 !=\cf16  \cf21 "quit"\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf21 "Hello,"\cf5 ,\cf16  name\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: Donna\cb1 \
\cb15 Hello, Donna\cb1 \
\cb15 Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: Vera\cb1 \
\cb15 Hello, Vera\cb1 \
\cb15 Enter a name or 'quit' to exit: quit\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Type merging\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When an expression involves values of different types, Mojo needs to statically determine the return type of the expression. This process is called type merging. By default, Mojo determines type merging based on implicit conversions. Individual structs can also define custom type merging behavior.\
The following code demonstrates type merging based on implicit conversions:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf22 \cb15 list\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf14 0.5\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 2\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 for\cf16  value \cf5 in\cf16  \cf22 list\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf16 value\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 0.5\cb1 \
\cb15 1.0\cb1 \
\cb15 2.0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Here, the list literal includes both float and integer literals, which materialize as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , respectively. Since 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be implicitly converted to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the result is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[Float64]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Here's an example of where type merging fails:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 a\cf5 :\cf16  Int \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 0\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 b\cf5 :\cf16  String \cf5 =\cf16  \cf21 "Hello"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 c \cf5 =\cf16  a \cf5 if\cf16  a \cf5 >\cf16  \cf14 0\cf16  \cf5 else\cf16  b   \cf6 # Error: value of type 'Int' is not compatible with\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                         \cf6 # value of type 'String'mojo\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this case, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can't be implicitly converted to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can't be implicitly converted to an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so type merging fails. This is the correct result: there's no way for Mojo to know what type you want 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 c
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to take. You can fix this by adding an explicit conversion:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 c \cf5 =\cf16  String\cf5 (\cf16 a\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 if\cf16  a \cf5 >\cf16  \cf14 0\cf16  \cf5 else\cf16  b\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Individual structs can define custom type merging logic by defining a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __merge_with__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dunder method. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 MyType\cf5 (\cf16 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  val\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __bool__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 )\cf16  raises \cf5 ->\cf16  Bool\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 val \cf5 >\cf16  \cf14 0\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  __merge_with__\cf5 [\cf16 other_type\cf5 :\cf16  type_of\cf5 (\cf16 Int\cf5 )](\cf16 self\cf5 )\cf16  raises \cf5 ->\cf16  Int\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 val\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf22 main\cf5 ()\cf16  raises\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     i \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 0\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     m \cf5 =\cf16  MyType\cf5 (\cf14 9\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf16 i \cf5 if\cf16  i \cf5 >\cf16  \cf14 0\cf16  \cf5 else\cf16  m\cf5 )\cf16   \cf6 # prints "9"\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If either type in the expression defines a custom 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __merge_with__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dunder for merging with the other type, this type takes precedence over any implicit conversions. (Note that the result type doesn't have to be either of the input types, it could be a third type.)\
A type can declare multiple 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __merge_with__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  overrides for different types.\
At a high level, the logic for merging two types goes like this:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Does either type define a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __merge_with__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method for the other type? If so, the returned value determines the target type.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
