\cf2 As an example, consider implementing support for all of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator dunder methods for a custom 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct. This shows supporting adding two 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instances as well as adding a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . We can support the case of having the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as the right-hand side argument by overloaded the definition of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __add__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . But to support the case of having the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as the left-hand side argument, we need to implement an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __radd__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method, because the built-in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type doesn't have an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __add__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that supports our custom 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

