\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 value \cf5 <\cf16  rhs\cf5 .\cf16 value\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # `__ne__`, `__le__`, `__gt__`, and `__ge__` have default implementations.\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Membership operator dunder methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 in
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 not in
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operators depend on a type implementing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __contains__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dunder method. Typically only collection types (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Set
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) implement this method. It should accept the right-hand side value as an argument and return a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  indicating whether the value is present in the collection or not.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Identity operator dunder methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is not
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operators compare the identity of two values.\
When a type conforms to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/identifiable/Identifiable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Identifiable}}, these operators call the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/identifiable/Identifiable/#__is__"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul __is__()}} method to determine whether the left-hand operand has the same identity as the right-hand operand. For example, you might compare raw values or use some other way to establish what "identity" means in your work.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is not
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator uses the automatically provided {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/identifiable/Identifiable/#__isnot__"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul __isnot__()}} method, which returns the logical negation of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __is__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Subscript and slicing dunder methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Subscripting and slicing typically apply only to sequential collection types, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Subscripting references a single element of a collection or a dimension of a multi-dimensional container, whereas slicing refers to a range of values. A type supports both subscripting and slicing by implementing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method for retrieving values and the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method for setting values.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Subscripting\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In the simple case of a one-dimensional sequence, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods should have signatures similar to this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 MySeq\cf5 [\cf22 type\cf5 :\cf16  Copyable\cf5 ]:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  idx\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  \cf22 type\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Return element at the given index\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __setitem__\cf5 (mut\cf16  self\cf5 ,\cf16  idx\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  value\cf5 :\cf16  \cf22 type\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Assign the element at the given index the provided value\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 It's also possible to support multi-dimensional collections, in which case you can implement both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods to accept multiple index arguments\'97or even variadic index arguments for arbitrary\'97dimension collections.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 MySeq\cf5 [\cf22 type\cf5 :\cf16  Copyable\cf5 ]:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # 2-dimension support\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  x_idx\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  y_idx\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  \cf22 type\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # Arbitrary-dimension support\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 *\cf16 indices\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  \cf22 type\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Slicing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You provide slicing support for a collection type also by implementing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods. But for slicing, instead of accepting an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  index (or indices, in the case of a multi-dimensional collection) you implement to methods to accept a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/builtin_slice/Slice/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Slice}} (or multiple 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Slice
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s in the case of a multi-dimensional collection).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 MySeq\cf5 [\cf22 type\cf5 :\cf16  Copyable\cf5 ]:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # Return a new MySeq with a subset of elements\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  span\cf5 :\cf16  Slice\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  Self\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Slice
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  contains three fields:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
start
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ): The starting index of the slice\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
end
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ): The ending index of the slice\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
step
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ): The step increment value of the slice.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because the start, end, and step values are all optional when using slice syntax, they are represented as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Slice
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . And if present, the index values might be negative representing a relative position from the end of the sequence. As a convenience, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Slice
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 indices()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that accepts a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 length
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value and returns a 3-tuple of "normalized" start, end, and step values for the given length, all represented as non-negative values. You can then use these normalized values to determine the corresponding elements of your collection being referenced.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 MySeq\cf5 [\cf22 type\cf5 :\cf16  Copyable\cf5 ]:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  size\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Return a new MySeq with a subset of elements\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  span\cf5 :\cf16  Slice\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  Self\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf16  start\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf16  end\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf16  step\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cb1 \
\cb15         start\cf5 ,\cf16  end\cf5 ,\cf16  step \cf5 =\cf16  span\cf5 .\cf16 indices\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 .\cf16 size\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 An example of implementing operators for a custom type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As an example of implementing operators for a custom Mojo type, let's create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct to represent a single complex number, with both the real and imaginary components stored as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values. We'll implement most of the arithmetic operators, the associated in-place assignment operators, the equality comparison operators, and a few additional convenience methods to support operations like printing complex values. We'll also allow mixing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values in arithmetic expressions to produce a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  result.\
This example builds our 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct incrementally. You can also find the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/tree/modular/v26.2/mojo/examples/operators"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul complete example in the public GitHub repo}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 Note that the Mojo standard library implements a parameterized {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/complex/complex/ComplexSIMD/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul ComplexSIMD}} struct that provides support for a basic set of arithmetic operators. However, our 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Complex
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  type will not be based on the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ComplexSIMD
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  struct or be compatible with it.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Implement lifecycle methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct is a simple value type with register passable numeric fields. It requires no special constructor or destructor behaviors. Conforming it to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/#register-passable"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul RegisterPassable}} and adopting 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  lets you assign the value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 Complex\cf5 (\cf16 RegisterPassable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  re\cf5 :\cf16  Float64\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  im\cf5 :\cf16  Float64\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This definition allows you to construct and use an instance without building an initializer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  c1 \cf5 =\cf16  Complex\cf5 (-\cf14 1.2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 6.5\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 t\cf21 "c1: Real: \{c1.re\}; Imaginary: \{c1.im\}"\cf5 )\cf16  \cf6 # c1: Real: -1.2; Imaginary: 6.5\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To allow you to construct a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance with just the real component, add a convenience initializer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf22 __init__\cf5 (out\cf16  self\cf5 ,\cf16  re\cf5 :\cf16  Float64\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     self\cf5 .\cf16 re \cf5 =\cf16  re\cb1 \
\cb15     self\cf5 .\cf16 im \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 0.0\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now you can create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance with just a real component:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  c2 \cf5 =\cf16  Complex\cf5 (-\cf14 1.2\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 t\cf21 "c2: Real: \{c2.re\}; Imaginary: \{c2.im\}"\cf5 )\cf16  \cf6 # c2: Real: -1.2; Imaginary: 0.0\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Implement the 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  trait\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To make it simpler to print 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values, let's implement the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Writable}} trait so that we can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor to generate a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representation of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value. You can find out more about these traits and their associated methods in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/#the-stringable-representable-and-writable-traits"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul The 
\fs28 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cb1  trait}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf22 Complex\cf5 (\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     Writable\cf5 ,\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     TrivialRegisterPassable\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 write_to\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 mut\cf16  writer\cf5 :\cf16  Some\cf5 [\cf16 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         writer\cf5 .\cf16 write\cf5 (\cf21 "("\cf5 ,\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 re\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 if\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 im \cf5 <\cf16  \cf14 0\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             writer\cf5 .\cf16 write\cf5 (\cf21 " - "\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf16 self\cf5 .\cf16 im\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 else:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             writer\cf5 .\cf16 write\cf5 (\cf21 " + "\cf5 ,\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 im\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         writer\cf5 .\cf16 write\cf5 (\cf21 "i)"\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now we can print a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value directly, and we can explicitly generate a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representation by passing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  which constructs a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from all the arguments passed to it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 c3 \cf5 =\cf16  Complex\cf5 (\cf14 3.14159\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf14 2.71828\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "c3 ="\cf5 ,\cf16  c3\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf16  msg \cf5 =\cf16  String\cf5 (\cf21 "The value is: "\cf5 ,\cf16  c3\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 msg\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 c3 = (3.1415899999999999 - 2.71828i)\cb1 \
\cb15 The value is: (3.1415899999999999 - 2.71828i)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Implement basic indexing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Indexing usually is supported only by collection types. But as an example, let's implement support for accessing the real component as index 0 and the imaginary component as index 1. We'll not implement slicing or variadic assignment for this example.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15     \cf6 # ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  idx\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 )\cf16  raises \cf5 ->\cf16  Float64\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 if\cf16  idx \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf14 0\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 return\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 re\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 elif\cf16  idx \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf14 1\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 return\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 im\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 else:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 raise\cf16  \cf21 "index out of bounds"\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf22 __setitem__\cf5 (mut\cf16  self\cf5 ,\cf16  idx\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  value\cf5 :\cf16  Float64\cf5 )\cf16  raises \cf5 ->\cf16  \cf14 None\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 if\cf16  idx \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf14 0\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             self\cf5 .\cf16 re \cf5 =\cf16  value\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 elif\cf16  idx \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf14 1\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             self\cf5 .\cf16 im \cf5 =\cf16  value\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 else:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 raise\cf16  \cf21 "index out of bounds"\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now let's try getting and setting the real and imaginary components of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value using indexing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 c2 \cf5 =\cf16  Complex\cf5 (\cf14 3.14159\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "c2[0]: \{\}; c2[1]: \{\}"\cf5 .\cf22 format\cf5 (\cf16 c2\cf5 [\cf14 0\cf5 ],\cf16  c2\cf5 [\cf14 1\cf5 ]))\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 c2\cf5 [\cf14 0\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 2.71828\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 c2\cf5 [\cf14 1\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 42\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf21 "c2[0] = 2.71828; c2[1] = 42; c2:"\cf5 ,\cf16  c2\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 c2[0]: 3.1415899999999999; c2[1]: 0.0\cb1 \
\cb15 c2[0] = 2.71828; c2[1] = 42; c2: (2.71828 + 42.0i)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Implement arithmetic operators\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now let's implement the dunder methods that allow us to perform arithmetic operations on 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values. (Refer to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_number"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Wikipedia page}} on complex numbers for a more in-depth explanation of the formulas for these operators.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Implement basic operators for 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  values\
