\fs32 \cf2 Next we'll implement the basic binary operators: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Dividing complex numbers is a bit tricky, so we'll also define a helper method called 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 norm()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to calculate the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norm_(mathematics)#Euclidean_norm_of_complex_numbers"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Euclidean norm}} of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance, which can also be useful for other types of analysis with complex numbers.\
For all of these dunder methods, the left-hand side operand is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and the right-hand side operand is passed as an argument. We return a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Complex
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value representing the result.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

